description: Redesign: store issues as real git notes again, so sync merges like git does instead of overwriting.
 
 Motivating bug (2026-10-10, two clones of one bare remote, both with setup-sync): an unpushed local comment is dropped by a plain git fetch, which prints (forced update) for refs/notes/issue-<id>; git pull does the same through the post-merge hook. Cause: the refspec +refs/notes/*:refs/notes/* writes the remote's refs straight over the local ones, as if git fetched with +refs/heads/*:refs/heads/*. Recoverable only from the reflog.
 
 Root cause: 0682bdf (2026-02-28) left the git notes API because notes were attached to HEAD and were orphaned by every new commit. It switched to hand-built commits, which cured the orphaning but dropped what notes give (git notes merge), hence the forced fetch. The notes-native fix is a stable anchor, not plumbing.
 
 Proposed design:
 1. One notes ref, refs/notes/issues. Each issue is a note on its own anchor, a blob written once with git hash-object -w (e.g. 'git-issue <title> <nonce>'); the issue id is the anchor's hash. Anchors never move, so nothing is orphaned. Linking to a commit can be a note on that commit.
 2. Plain git works: git notes --ref=issues show <anchor>, git notes append for comments, git log refs/notes/issues for history, notes.displayRef to show issues in git log.
 3. Sync as branches do: fetch +refs/notes/issues:refs/notes/remotes/<remote>/issues (a tracking ref), then git notes --ref=issues merge refs/notes/remotes/<remote>/issues. Comments are append-only (cat_sort_uniq); field changes on both sides use -s manual and a git-issue field-level resolve before git notes merge --commit. Push without force, so a diverged notes ref is rejected like a diverged branch.
 4. Edges (deps) as notes on the anchors too, or one index note, merged the same way.
 5. Migration: replay each refs/notes/issue-<id> history into a note on an anchor that keeps <id>, as the format-1 upgrade did; keep reading the old layout until migrated.
 
 Acceptance: the two-clone repro keeps both comments after fetch/pull; a both-sides status change is reported as a conflict, not dropped; a diverged push is rejected; git notes show works on an issue id.
